#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064992} :activity/id #uuid "8a6775e9-2229-5837-a376-b69fe905d97e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048123 :topic/id #uuid "80ecc4ad-075a-51a6-a1fc-72dc61a2444d" :topic/math-academy-id 313} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064992 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065347 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the graph of the function $y=f(x)$ on the closed interval $[0,7]$ shown below.\n\n![](images/39/39851b8fc5658ccfd92711eb9fe7a00a730300a3bf2e635911bdd51ea69de37a.png)\n\nThe **critical points** of $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ where $f'(x)=0$ or $f'(x)$ doesn't exist. Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal or where the tangent does not exist, respectively.\n\nIn the given graph, we can see that \n\n- at $x=2,$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have $f'(x)=0,$ while\n\n- at $x=4,$ there is a sharp corner, so $f'(x)$ doesn't exist.\n\nSo, there are two critical points, and their $x$-coordinates are $x=2$ and $x=4.$ \n\nIn particular, a critical point is called a **stationary point** if $f'(x)=0.$ So here, the points $x=2$ and $x=4$ are both critical points, but only the point at $x=2$ is a stationary point.\n\n![](images/4e/4e91994fa85a068ef847cbd82dc176569fd15ab2487dfff6936280e9a1deb634.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "36584e6c-f116-5ad4-bb03-576b0f7e9c0e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2660 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "02673d5b-6611-5854-bca0-ff7445f2f3c3" :step/math-academy-id 12782 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065158}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064993 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064162 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064044 :question/id #uuid "2d9fc36f-8d8f-5e57-9ff0-28a032e71980" :question/math-academy-id "e-3726"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a595bc07-ad64-537d-be42-06144814b97d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050033}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064801} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064835} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329920} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329921} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402902} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402909}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema of a Function Given Its Graph"} :step/id #uuid "2b2aa51f-4124-5395-afb4-05149fddd969" :step/math-academy-id 12789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065158 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064273 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064116 :question/id #uuid "97897d09-3dc9-5e35-8f56-d44f54c542cc" :question/math-academy-id "e-6169"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b5ea4a7-acc4-5825-b6c5-b6c87e6a1674" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050711}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235483} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403633} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403640}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Critical and Stationary Points of a Function Given Its Graph"} :step/id #uuid "589482bf-a622-576c-8b59-1c5332c13087" :step/math-academy-id 23042 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065159}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065159 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065374 :tutorial/content "Some critical points are **local extrema**. There are two types of local extrema:\n\n- A **local minimum** is the lowest point of a valley in a graph. Equivalently, $x=a$ is a local minimum if for $x$ values \"close\" to $x=a,$ we have $f(x) \\geq f(a)$.\n\n- A **local maximum** is the highest point of a peak in a graph. Equivalently, $x=a$ is a local maximum if for $x$ values \"close\" to $x=a,$ we have $f(x) \\leq f(a)$.\n\n![](images/d6/d6fbdf74b282bd5b8172fcc7927f84bbdfd050a717794f3b40c8e58b83d7b87e.png)\n\nOn the graph above, the critical point at $x=4$ is a local minimum since it is the lowest point of a valley. Likewise, the critical point at $x=6$ is a local maximum since it is the highest point of a peak. But the critical point at $x=2$ is not a local extremum since it is neither a peak nor a valley.\n\nThe **global extrema** of a function in an interval are given by the lowest or highest points of the function's graph in the whole interval.\n\n- The lowest point is the **global minimum**, whereas\n\n- the highest point is the **global maximum**.\n\nHere, the global minimum is at $x=4$, and the global maximum is at $x=0.$\n\n![](images/8a/8adb83da866dae1cd122b5271b2cb9036c72a389eaf1c48148888140bda576ac.png)\n\n\"Local\" extrema are the lowest or highest points compared to points that are very close to them. On the other hand, \"global\" extrema are the lowest/highest points of the graph over its entire domain." :tutorial/id #uuid "05856315-caeb-577d-9dea-b5e5f8830820" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3898 :tutorial/title "Local and Global Extrema of a Function"} :step/id #uuid "b952eadc-3fe2-5be0-8961-f9af3dc6fcd5" :step/math-academy-id 23043 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064993}}] :activity/title "Global vs. Local Extrema and Critical Points" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064409}]}
